this is what google gemini had to say about the code: 1. The "Broker Ban" Risk (Critical)
The biggest danger in this script is how it handles the Runner Mode (trailing stop). Currently, the bot attempts to move your Stop Loss every single time the price moves in your favor, even if it’s just by one cent.
Because Bitcoin fluctuates constantly, this will cause your bot to send hundreds or thousands of "Modify Order" requests to your broker in a very short time. Most brokers will flag this as "hyper-activity" or server spamming and will either disable your auto-trading or freeze your account to protect their servers. You need to tell the bot to only move the Stop Loss if the price has moved by a significant chunk (like $50 or $100) since the last update.
2. The "Hidden Fee" Trap
Your bot calculates profit based purely on the distance the price has moved (Gross Profit). It does not check how much money you paid in commissions or swap fees to open the trade.
In Crypto scalping, commissions are often much higher than in Forex. A trade might look like it is in profit on the chart, but once you deduct the fees, you are actually still losing money. If your bot’s "Tier 1" logic locks in a small profit without checking these fees, you could be locking in a guaranteed net loss.
3. Dangerous Position Sizing
The math the bot uses to calculate how much to buy (Lot Size) relies on a specific piece of data from the broker called "Tick Value."
On many MetaTrader crypto brokers, this specific data point is often configured incorrectly or fluctuates wildly. If the broker reports this value as zero or a weird number, your bot creates a division error that could result in it opening a maxed-out position (the largest trade allowed), risking far more of your account than the 1% you intended.
4. You Are Being Too Slow
When the bot tries to open a trade and fails (perhaps due to a busy market), you have it set to wait 500 milliseconds (half a second) before trying again.
In Bitcoin scalping, half a second is an eternity. If you are trading a breakout, the price can move $50 or $100 in that time. By the time the bot retries, the entry price is gone. This delay needs to be much shorter (almost instant) to catch the move.
5. Round Number Logic is Backwards
Your strategy currently reduces your risk (bets smaller) when the price is near a "Round Number" (like $90,000 or $100,000).
While this makes sense for a "Bounce" strategy, it is bad for a "Breakout" strategy. In Bitcoin, when the price finally breaks through a major round number, it often explodes in that direction. By betting smaller here, you are handicapping yourself right when the biggest potential winner is about to happen.
6. The Spread Filter is Too Loose
You have the filter set to allow a spread of roughly 0.15%. On a $100,000 Bitcoin, that is a $150 spread.
If you are scalping for a small target (e.g., $300 profit), paying a $150 spread means you are giving away 50% of your potential profit just to enter the trade. This makes it mathematically very difficult to be profitable in the long run. You generally want the spread to be a much smaller fraction of your target.
7. Slippage Calculation
The bot tries to calculate "slippage" (how much bad pricing you accept) using "Points." In Forex, a point is standard. In Bitcoin, a "point" varies wildly between brokers (some see it as $1, others as $0.01). If the bot miscalculates this, it might accidentally tell the broker to accept any price, causing you to get filled at a terrible rate during a volatility spike.

